        },
        "text_8fa3ye": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "130ch",
            "richtext": "<h4><strong>Understanding Indoor Humidity and Why It Matters</strong></h4><p></p><p>Indoor humidity refers to the amount of moisture in the air, measured as relative humidity. This percentage reflects how much moisture the air holds compared to what it could hold at the same temperature.</p><p>When humidity is too low, common effects include dry skin, irritated sinuses, sore throats, static electricity, and disrupted sleep. Over time, very dry air can also affect wood furniture, flooring, and musical instruments.</p><p>The ideal indoor humidity range for comfort and health typically falls between <strong>30 and 50 percent</strong>, depending on the season and climate.</p><h4><strong>Why Is Humidity Harder to Increase in Winter?</strong></h4><p></p><p>Cold air holds far less moisture than warm air. When cold outdoor air enters your home and is heated, its relative humidity drops dramatically.</p><p>For example, outdoor air at 30°F can hold less than half the moisture of indoor air at 70°F. Once heated, that air becomes extremely dry, meaning your humidifier must work continuously to replace moisture lost through drafts and normal air exchange.</p><p>This is why humidity levels often plateau in winter, even after running a humidifier overnight.</p><h4><strong>Why Does My Humidifier Only Raise Humidity a Few Percent?</strong></h4><p></p><p>A small increase does not mean your humidifier is underperforming.</p><p>In very dry environments, even a <strong>5 to 10 percent increase</strong> in relative humidity represents a meaningful amount of added moisture. The humidifier adds water continuously, but the environment removes it at the same time. This balance limits visible changes, even though the unit is working as designed.</p>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
        "image_caption_Xi6zz4": {
          "type": "image_caption",
          "settings": {
