            "max_width_desktop": "1096px",
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/Blog_image_1.jpg",
            "image_desktop": "shopify://shop_images/Blog_image_1.jpg",
            "caption": "",
            "caption_style": "p6",
            "product": "2-in-1-pro-purify-humidify",
            "product_image_override": "shopify://shop_images/2in1_1.png"
          }
        },
        "text_xyBxGP": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "130ch",
            "richtext": "<h4><strong>Why Do I Need to Refill the Water Tank More Often in Winter?</strong></h4><p></p><p>Refilling your humidifier more frequently during winter is usually a sign that it is working properly.</p><p>Dry air absorbs moisture more readily, causing the humidifier to use water faster. Increased water usage means moisture is actively being added to the air, not wasted. In more humid seasons, the same tank may last much longer because the air cannot absorb as much additional moisture.</p><h4><strong>Why Doesn’t My Humidity Reading Match Another Hygrometer?</strong></h4><p></p><p>It is normal for humidity readings to vary between devices. Sensors can differ by <strong>plus or minus five percent or more</strong> depending on sensor quality, placement, airflow, height from the floor, and proximity to the humidifier output.</p><p>For best accuracy, place sensors away from vents and windows, avoid placing them directly next to the humidifier, and allow several hours for readings to stabilize after adjustments.</p><p>Small differences do not indicate a performance issue.</p><h4><strong>What Humidity Level Should I Realistically Expect?</strong></h4><p></p><p>Achievable indoor humidity depends on the season and climate:</p><ul><li><strong>30 to 40 percent</strong> in winter or very dry climates</li><li><strong>40 to 50 percent</strong> in milder seasons</li></ul><p></p><p>Trying to maintain levels above 50 percent in winter is not recommended, as it can increase condensation on windows and walls. A humidifier is designed to improve comfort, not override extreme environmental conditions.</p><h4><strong>How Can I Improve Humidifier Performance?</strong></h4><p></p><p>If humidity levels remain low, these expert tips can help:</p><ul><li>Close doors and windows</li><li>Reduce drafts where possible</li><li>Place the humidifier centrally in the room</li><li>Avoid placing it near vents or heaters</li><li>Use higher settings during very dry periods</li><li>Allow several hours, or overnight, for humidity to stabilize</li></ul><p></p><p>In extremely dry homes or larger spaces, using multiple humidifiers may be necessary to achieve consistent comfort.</p><h4><strong>What This Means for Your Home</strong></h4><p></p><p>If your humidifier is not reaching the exact number you set, it does not mean it is broken. Indoor humidity is influenced by climate, temperature, airflow, and seasonal conditions. Understanding what is realistic, and how your environment affects moisture, can help you set better expectations and improve everyday comfort.</p><p>Effective humidification is not about chasing a specific number. It is about creating healthier, more comfortable indoor air for the way you live.</p>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
